enchilada: the wall clock survives reboots (RTC offset stored on disk)

The PMIC RTC only counts and is never written, so every cold boot started at the image's build time (sweetepoch) until something set the clock, and HTTPS (certificate dates) failed meanwhile; the flashed phone came up 58 minutes behind. setUTCTimeOfDay (timed/NTP, Settings) now stores the offset of UNIX time to the RTC counter in the block just before the console log on userdata ("SWEETRTC", when it moved by more than 2 s); once the partition is ready the stored offset is read back, used unless the RTC counter went backwards since (an RTC reset), and the calendar re-read (clock_initialize_calendar).

--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_ufs.c
